by rela589n at 2026-09-26T12:07:12Z

> [Validation::createCallable()](https://github.com/symfony/symfony/blob/69e66e32171baf8d3ccb11e6d5b50583860d622b/src/Symfony/Component/Validator/Validation.php#L31) is variadic, the following is equivalent:
>
> ```php
> $title = $value |> Validation::createCallable(
>    new NotBlank(),
>    new Length(min: 4, max: 255),
> );
> ```

It's not. This snippet causes both constraints to execute at once.
On the other hand, the `|>` operator won't proceed unless `new NotBlank()`  succeeds:

```php
$title = $value
  |> new NotBlank() // throws
  |> new Length(min: 4, max: 255);
```

It makes no difference for the valid values, but for the invalid `''` it issues two messages:
- "This value should not be blank."
- "This value is too short. It should have 4 characters or more."

It is necessary to apply `new Sequentially()` there to make `NotBlank` trigger first:

```php
$title = $value |> Validation::createCallable(new Sequentially([
    new NotBlank(),
    new Length(min: 4, max: 255),
]));
```

